- There are dynamic socials classes. In the beginning, there are: senate, equites and plebs. Later on, senate will decline and clergy class will rise.
- Each class has two properties: loyalty and influence. Loyalty ranges from 0 - 100 (initial = 100); Influence ranges from 0 - Infinity (initial = 100); Loyalty is a resource (and the player spend other sources to increase it), Influence is a stats that needs to be managed.
- Clergy class will have 0 influence in the beginning (because they are now spawned yet)
- All actions look at relative Influence, which is the percentage of that class' influence: Influence% = Class Influence / All Class Influence.
- The player can issue edicts. Each edict provides gameplay bonuses and one supporting class and one opposing class.
- An edict can only be issued if the supporting class has >= 50 Loyalty and >= 50% Influence.
- After issuing an edict, supporting classes gain +10 Influence. Opposing classes lose -10 Loyalty.
- An edict lasts for 5 years and can expire early when supporting class has < 50 Loyalty.
- A new edict can be issued every 3 years. An active edict cannot be issued again. Different edicts can be active at the same time and its effect can stack.
- If a social class has >= 50% Influence, a negative effect is applied; If a social class has < 50 Loyalty, a negative effect is applied; These two negative effects can stack.
- "Curry Favor" action: cost gold, increase a class' loyalty, 2-year cooldown
- "Arrange Marriage" action: marry a family member, increase a class' loyalty, uncommon
- "Elevate" action: cost admin point, increase a class' influence by 20%, shared 5-year cooldown with "Suppress"
- "Suppress" action: cost military point, reduce a class' influence by 20%, shared 5-year cooldown with "Elevate"

